{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 RCA — confirmed defect (no repair performed yet)\n\nProven root cause: every cumulative-counter block in infra/scripts/observability.sh persists its offset as soon as the source total is observed, before aws cloudwatch put-metric-data acknowledges delivery (replication 101-108; voter 130-138; merge 153-159; deploy 174-180; shared deferral/interrupt helper 203-212; G2P 252-260). The persisted offset is an acknowledgement cursor, so a failed publish followed by the same cumulative total computes zero and permanently excludes the failed positive delta. The ticket omitted G2P; it is the same copied construct.\n\nDiscriminating runtime proof: a whole-script PATH-shim experiment parameterized all seven metric streams across six code shapes. Each began with offset 2 and source total 7; the targeted first AWS call exited 23. In every case the script exited 0 and the offset became 7. The next successful run published value 0, while retaining offset 2 would publish 5. All 9 probes passed, including both shared-helper metrics, G2P, and a reset control (offset 10/source 7 produced failed 7 then successful 0). Removing only 2>/dev/null || true still produced 5 then 0, proving suppression hides the loss but is not a second necessary cause. A zero delta has no positive count to acknowledge and is a negative control, not a separate behavior change. Temporary instrumentation was removed after the experiment.\n\nDefect legitimacy: defect. The original stories require observed replication, voter, merge-path, deploy, review-interrupt, and G2P failures to publish metrics and trip their alarms (spiny-micro-ladle; cogent-strut-kid; elfish-chair-lug; gall-plume-adder; equestrian-clueless-cowbird; smoky-flare-slug). Current Terraform contracts define these as per-period new-error counts. No authority permits discarding a detected count after transient CloudWatch failure.\n\nCaller-dependency gate: expected behavior is that offsets advance only after the matching AWS call succeeds; on failure they remain byte-identical so the next run retries the accumulated delta. The script is installed by install-observability.sh/systemd and refreshed by autodeploy; tests and runbooks consume its metrics but no caller depends on premature acknowledgement. All affected metrics share the same contract. No behavioral-dependency blocker."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786300511594168001,"uuid":"5b151e25-1520-4df1-aec3-091cd0c58630"}